Your router commit (2e73129) fixed the __init__ half: bind_mode now
defaults to "lan", _lan_enabled gets computed, Transport binds
0.0.0.0. That's the part that unbreaks LAN messaging, and it works.

But _lan_enabled was never actually USED anywhere. start() still called
self._discovery.start() unconditionally, so `network_bind=host` —
the explicit "I don't want to be on the LAN" setting — still broadcast
your presence and your real LAN IP to every device on the network,
while Transport listened on loopback only. Every peer that discovered
you would then get ECONNREFUSED. That's the same incoherent half-state
the fix exists to eliminate, just inverted: instead of "discoverable
but unreachable" being the default, it became "discoverable but
unreachable" for anyone who explicitly opted out.

A half-applied fix to a two-sided invariant is worse than no fix,
because everything compiles, every existing test passes, and the flag
sitting there in __init__ makes it look done.

Completed it:
- start() only starts Discovery when _lan_enabled, and logs plainly
  when it doesn't.
- stop() mirrors that guard — Discovery.stop() joins its two threads
  unconditionally, so calling it on a never-started Discovery raises
  RuntimeError. Verified that's really the behavior rather than
  assuming it.

Also corrected an assertion in test_security_fixes.py that was still
enforcing the OLD loopback-by-default behavior. The test wasn't broken
by the fix — it was encoding a decision we deliberately reversed, so
leaving it would have meant the suite actively defended the bug. It now
asserts the invariant that actually matters: discovery and reachability
agree, whichever mode you're in. (Original "Fix #3" reasoning was mine
and it was wrong for this codebase specifically: Discovery always
broadcast your real IP regardless, so loopback-only Transport bought no
privacy — it only broke messaging. Your bug report is what surfaced it.)

New test in test_multi_profile.py covering the invariant directly,
using a genuinely non-loopback local address so "reachable on LAN" and
"reachable on loopback" are actually distinguishable — an earlier
version of this check quietly passed for the wrong reason because
gethostbyname() resolved to 127.0.0.1 in a container. Checks both
modes, both directions, plus the stop()-without-start() crash case.

test_multi_profile.py 13/13, test_security_fixes.py 29/29, full suite
still green.
---
 core/network/router.py | 19 +++++++++--
 test_multi_profile.py  | 71 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 test_security_fixes.py | 31 ++++++++++++------
 3 files changed, 110 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/core/network/router.py b/core/network/router.py
index 13081bd..f47c9cc 100644
--- a/core/network/router.py
+++ b/core/network/router.py
@@ -119,7 +119,18 @@ class Node:
 
     def start(self):
         self._transport.start()
-        self._discovery.start()
+        if self._lan_enabled:
+            self._discovery.start()
+        else:
+            # Without this, network_bind=host produced the exact incoherent
+            # half-state the bind_mode fix above exists to prevent, just
+            # inverted: Discovery still broadcasts your presence and real
+            # LAN IP to everyone, while Transport listens on loopback only,
+            # so every peer that discovers you gets connection-refused when
+            # they try to reach you. Opting out of LAN has to mean opting
+            # out of both halves, or it isn't opting out of anything.
+            log.info("[node] LAN discovery disabled (network_bind=host) — "
+                      "not broadcasting or listening for LAN peers")
         if self._dht:
             bootstrap = self._config.get_setting("dht_bootstrap", [])
             self._dht.start(bootstrap_addrs=bootstrap)
@@ -139,7 +150,11 @@ class Node:
         log.info("[node] started — user_id: %s", self._identity["user_id"][:16])
 
     def stop(self):
-        self._discovery.stop()
+        # Discovery.stop() joins its two threads unconditionally, which
+        # would raise if they were never started — so this has to mirror
+        # the guard in start() exactly.
+        if self._lan_enabled:
+            self._discovery.stop()
         self._transport.stop()
         if self._dht:
             self._dht.stop()
diff --git a/test_multi_profile.py b/test_multi_profile.py
index bda0fe3..a8b1854 100644
--- a/test_multi_profile.py
+++ b/test_multi_profile.py
@@ -141,11 +141,82 @@ for name, tport, wport, pw in {profiles_spec!r}:
         shutil.rmtree(tmp_home, ignore_errors=True)
         shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(repo, "storage"), ignore_errors=True)
 
+    test_bind_mode_coherence()
